## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Article history:  Received 10 November 2021  Revised 23 December 2021  Accepted 27 December 2021  Available online 29 December 2021
Keywords:  \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) Reduction  Electrocatalysis  Bismuth MOF  Surface Decoration  Indium
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derivatives have shown great potential as electrocatalysts, in virtue of their ease of functionalization and abundance of active sites. Here, we report a series of indium-doped bismuth MOF-derived composites \( \left(\mathrm{Biln}_{\mathrm{X}}-\mathrm{Y}@\mathrm{C}\right) \) for the direct conversion of carbon dioxide \( \left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right) \) to hydrocarbon derivatives. Amongst the catalysts studied, \( \mathrm{Biln}_{5}-500@\mathrm{C} \) demonstrated high selectivity for the production of formate and intrinsic activity in a wide potential window, ranging from \( -1.16 \) to \( -0.76 \) V vs. RHE \( \left(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}\right) \). At \( -0.86 \) \( \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}} \), the Faradaic efficiency and total current density were determined as \( 97.5\% \) and \( -13.5 \) mA cm\(^{-2}\), respectively. In addition, a 15-h stability test shows no obvious signs of deactivation. Complementary density functional theory (DFT) calculations revealed that the In-doped \( \mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) are the predominant active centers for HCOOH production in the reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) under the action of the \( \mathrm{Biln}_{\mathrm{X}}-\mathrm{Y}@\mathrm{C} \) catalyst. This work provides new detailed insights into reaction mechanism, and selectivity for reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) via MOFs, which are expected to inspire and guide the design of novel, selective and efficient catalysts.
