## 1. Introduction
Carbon dioxide \( \left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right) \) is the most abundant greenhouse gas in the troposphere with annual global emission of the order of gigatons. \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) is emitted in the atmosphere primarily by anthropogenic activities such as exploitation of fossil fuels and deforestation. The
increasing reliance on fossil fuels has led to a rapid increase in the global emission of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) (and other greenhouse gasses) in the span of a few decades, causing detrimental, long-lasting impact on global climate [1-3]. In this context, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) capture and utilization can represent an important approach to mitigate the emission of this greenhouse gas by converting \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to added-value products such as low-carbon fuels [2,4,5]. Notwithstanding its potential environmental and economic benefit, conversion of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) is inherently challenging due to the remarkable thermodynamic stability of this molecule. Thus, high activation barriers hinder \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reaction and decomposition and have rendered many \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) conversion efforts vain, especially under ambient conditions. Several technologies have been developed in past decade to achieve this challenging task. Most of the current strategies consist in photocatalytic or electrocatalytic reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \), or a combination of the two, to produce hydrocarbons and their derivates, such as short chain alcohols and organic acids [6]. Electrocatalytic reduction of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) \( \left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER}\right) \) employs a catalyst such as a metal (oxide) or carbon-based material, to promote a series of electron (and proton) transfers which ultimately lead to the formation of one or more products. \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) is recognized as a promising strategy for several reasons. Firstly, it can be carried out under ambient temperature and pressure, and the extent of the reaction process can be carefully controlled by adjusting the applied voltage and current [7,8] to produce one- and two-carbon products \( \left(\mathsf{C}_{1}\right. \) and \( \left.\mathsf{C}_{2}\right) \) in carbon–neutral cycle at a low consumption rate [9-12]. Despite the design of many classes of promising electrocatalysts, the substantial thermodynamic barriers towards products formation, which lead to high overpotentials and low energy efficiency, still limit the wide implementation of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \). The optimal catalyst for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) should display low overpotentials as well as high selectivity towards the formation of a specific product. One class of promising catalysts is represented by bismuth-based materials which constitute efficient, benign and nontoxic materials, capable of reducing \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) to produce formic acid with highly-selectivity [13,14]. Furthermore, these materials have relatively high overpotentials for hydrogen evolution, a reduction reaction that competes and limits \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \), and also display modest CO adsorption energies, which allow the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) to selectively form formic acid with the least CO poisoning [15,16]. Formic acid is an important material used in many industrial processes [17,18] and can serve as fuel for fuel cells [19,20]. While many procedures to produce formic acid may be established, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) could still represent an efficient and convenient route for this purpose. Previous studies have demonstrated that electrochemical \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction to produce formic acid (or even CO) via a the two-electron transfer process is economically feasible [21-25]. Among the various \( \mathrm{C}_{1} \) and \( \mathrm{C}_{2}-\mathrm{C}_{4} \) hydrocarbons, formic acid possesses the highest added value per KWh of electrical energy input \( (0.43\,\mathbb{S}\,\mathrm{KWh}^{-1}) \) [26], which makes it one of the desired products of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \), if conversion is achieved under mild conditions and high energy conversion efficiency.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are periodic, porous, crystalline materials formed by the self-assembly of (in)organic ligands and metal ions (metal clusters) [27-32]. MOFs have several desirable characteristics and properties which can drive \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) – e.g. diverse metal sources, structural defects, and interactions of metal atoms and adjacent heteroatoms. MOFs are easy to synthesize (e.g. by self-assembly coordination methods) and functionalize, and their structural properties, such as pore size, can be easily tuned. These excellent structural characteristics make MOFs attractive for a wide variety of fields and applications - most notably in catalysis - due to the abundance of exposed catalytic active sites. [33-36]. MOFs also can be used as templates or precursors to construct porous carbon uniformly-coated metal or metal oxide nanocomposites under calcination conditions [37,38]. Such MOF derivatives are also known to have unique physical and chemical properties
functionalities (e.g., unique optical, electrical, magnetic and catalytic properties), which make them highly applicable in the field of catalysis [39-43]. Yao et al. synthesized MOF-derived Cu-based catalyst HKUST-1 for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) to ethylene [44]; during which, the copper catalysts underwent two consecutive reductions from \( \mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \) to \( \mathrm{Cu}^{+} \) (fast) and finally to \( \mathrm{Cu}^{0} \) (slow), followed by surface reconstruction towards a \( \mathrm{Cu@Cu_{x}O} \) core@shell composite. In this instance, the abundance of \( \mathrm{Cu}^{+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{0} \) on the catalyst surface facilitated \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) activation and \( \mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C} \) coupling to form \( \mathrm{C_{n}} \) (with \( \mathtt{n}\geq2 \)), while inhibiting the formation of \( \mathrm{C}_{1} \) by-product. The catalyst exhibited a good Faradaic efficiency \( \mathrm{(FE_{C_{2}H_{4}}},\,51\pm3\% \) at \( -1.58\;\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}) \) towards ethylene, with \( 20\ \mathrm{~h~} \) of stable operation. This study reported an excellent example that illustrates how MOFs can be engineered to serve as efficient and durable catalysts. Some p-block post-transition metals, such as Bi, In, Sn and Pb, are well known to be very selective towards the formation of formate during \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \). Yang et al. [45] prepared leaf-shaped bismuth nanosheets with mixed Bi/Bi-O interfaces (BiNSs) derived from CAU-17 MOF, which demonstrated high selectivity and durability towards formate for over \( 10\,\mathrm{h} \) with a constant FE of over \( 90\% \). However, the electrolysis potentials in the \( \mathrm{KHCO}_{3} \) electrolyte were relatively high \( (-1.14\mathrm{~V~} \) vs. RHE @ \( 128\,\mathrm{\mA} \)), which can be further improved. Previous studies showed that introduction of another metal within the Bi-based catalyst can increase the intrinsic activity of the catalyst for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \). Wen et al. have prepared bimetallic Bi-Sn composites which exhibited and excellent selectivity for forming formate with a FE of \( 96\% \) and high production rates. The remarkable selectivity was attributed to an increased binding energy of the \( \mathrm{HCOO} \ast \) intermediate to the surface [46].
In this work, we designed a novel MOF-based catalyst for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER} \) to formate with Indium (In) doping to improve the formate selectivity due to the great potential of In. The resulting catalysts \( \left(\mathrm{BiIn}_{\mathrm{X}}-\right. \) \( \mathsf{Y@C} \) with \( \mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}\,=\,0,\,3,\,5,\,7 \) and \( \Upsilon=400,\,500 \) and 600) were prepared as to contain different ratios of Indium with the purpose of exploring the effect of its content on the performance and selectivity of the catalyst. Furthermore, calcination at different temperatures allowed constructing and characterizing porous carbon coated metal or metal oxide nanocomposites of higher catalytic performance. The unique composition and structure of \( \mathrm{BiIn}_{\mathrm{X}}-\mathrm{Y@C} \) materials allowed them to exhibit excellent activity, selectivity, and stability.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
\( \mathrm{Bi}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) \( (>99\%) \), \( \mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) \( (99.99\%) \), 1, 3, 5-benzenetricarboxylic acid \( \left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC},\,>\!99\%\right) \), and methanol \( (99.9\%) \) were purchased from Adamas. N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) \( (\geq\!99.5\%) \), and isopropanol \( (\geq\!99.7\%) \) were acquired from Greagent. 5 wt.% Nafion solution was obtained from Hesen. Sinopharm Chemical supplied \( \mathrm{KHCO}_{3} \) \( (99.5\%) \).
## 2.2. Syntheses procedures
Bi-MOF was synthesized according to the procedure described elsewhere [47-49]. Briefly, \( 150~\mathrm{mg} \) of \( \mathrm{Bi}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) and \( 750~\mathrm{mg} \) of \( \mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC} \) were mixed with \( 48~\mathrm{mL} \) of DMF and \( 12~\mathrm{mL} \) of methanol. The mixture was transferred to a \( 100\,\mathrm{\mL} \) stainless steel Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at \( 120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 24~\mathrm{h} \). The resulting white products were centrifuged, washed with methanol and ethanol three times, and vacuum-dried at \( 80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12~\mathrm{h} \).
Bi-Y@C samples were synthesized by pyrolyzing Bi-MOF at \( \mathbf{\Delta}\mathbf{V}=400 \), 500, and \( 600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 2~\mathrm{{h}} \) under \( \mathrm{O} \) flow. The samples were
then cooled at room temperature and used without any further treatment.
\(\mathrm{Biln}_{\mathrm{X}^{-}}\mathrm{Y@C}\) samples were obtained by first dissolving corresponding \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) amounts in \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol to achieve doping level X equal to 3, 5, and \(7\%\) . Subsequently, \(0.2\mathrm{~g~}\) of BiMOF was added, and the mixture was then stirred for \(4~\mathrm{{h}}\) . The resulting precipitate was filtered and vacuum-dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . In this state, with the addition amount from 3, 5 to \(7\%\) , the actual mass fraction of In in the series of samples is \(1.51\%\) , \(1.75\%\) and \(1.95\%\) respectively (See Table. S1 for details). The resulting powders were then pyrolyzed under the same conditions as described above.
## 2.3. Preparation of the electrodes
All working electrodes were catalyst-supported gas diffusion electrodes (GDE). To prepare these GDEs, a few pieces of carbon paper were sonicated in deionized water first, and then in ethanol. A third sonication was performed again in deionized water. These will serve as catalyst support after drying. \(15~\mathrm{mg}\) of a catalyst was placed in a flask, and \(1000~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of isopropanol and \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(5~\mathrm{wt\%}\) Nafion solution were added to it. The mixture was sonicated for \(30~\mathrm{min}\) to obtain a uniform dispersion, and was then ready to be deposited on the catalyst support prepared above. To achieve this, the carbon paper was placed on a heated platform (at a constant temperature, \(30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), and the catalytic dispersion was added to its surface dropwise. After drying, a catalyst-loaded GDE was ready. Each GDE was \(2\times2~\mathrm{cm}\) and contained \(3.75~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}\) of the specific catalyst.
## 2.4. Characterization techniques
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was performed using Zeiss Sigma 300 instrument operated at \(10.0\,\mathrm{kV}\), while a high-resolution (HR) JEOL JEM 2100F setup was used for transmission electron microscopy (TEM). X-ray diffraction (XRD) was acquired using the Rigaku Ultima IV instrument and Cu-Ka radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were recorded by a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha instrument equipped with Al Ka monochromatized radiation and calibrated using C 1 s energy equal to \(284.6~\mathrm{eV}\). Thermogravimetry (Fig. S1) was performed under an \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere by a TGA5500 setup at a \(5\,\mathrm{~K~min}^{-1}\) heating rate \((30\sim700\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area was characterized by nitrogen adsorption in ASAP 2460 gas adsorption apparatus. The Raman measurements were carried out at room temperature by confocal micro-Raman spectrometer of inVia Qontor. Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectrometer of Brulker explored the signal of oxygen vacancy in materials. The metal content was obtained by Agilent 511 through Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry (ICP-MS) test.
## 2.5. Electrochemical tests
An H-type electrolytic cell and CHI 760E electrochemical workstation were used during electrochemical testing. The used an H-type electrolytic cell (consisting of a three-electrode setup with Pt wire in the anode chamber as a counter electrode, GDE as a working electrode, \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) system in the cathode chamber as a reference electrode and \(0.5\,\mathrm{~M~KHCO}_{3}\) as an electrolyte) and CHI 760E electrochemical workstation. The cathode and anode chambers were isolated from each other by a Nafion117 ion exchange membrane. Prior to the tests, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) was bubbled through the electrolyte in the cathode chamber until \(\mathrm{pH}=8.4\) was achieved. After that, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was bubbled through the electrolyte solution to saturate it with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\); Saturation was considered complete when the measured \(\mathrm{pH}\) became equal to 7.4.
The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER}\) was monitored by cyclic and linear sweep voltammetry (CV and LSV, respectively) as well as chronoamperometry (CA) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). The initial potentials, which were measured versus \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) \(\left(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}\right)\), were converted relative to the reversible hydrogen electrode potential \(\left(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}\right)\) as shown in Equation (1):
\[
V_{RHE}=V_{Ag/AgCl}+V_{0-Ag/AgCl\cdot vs\cdot NHE}+0.059pH
\]
where \(\mathrm{V}_{0-\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}\) vs. NHE is \(0.199~\mathrm{V}\) at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [50].
Liquid \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER}\) products were analyzed by ion chromatography using a IC1820 instrument (manufactured by Shanghai Sunny Hengping Scientific Instrument Co.) equipped with a Shodex IC SI-50-4E column. The gas products were characterized by GC-2014C gas chromatograph.
The Faradaic efficiency (FE) of formate \(\left(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{HCOO}^{-}}\right)\) was calculated by Eq. (2) [51]:
\[
FE_{HCOO^{-}}(\%)=\frac{2\times\mathfrak{n}\times\mathrm{F}}{\mathrm{Q}}\times100
\]
where 2 is the number of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) electrons participating in formate formation; F is the Faraday constant equal to \(96,500\mathrm{C\mol}^{-1}\); n is the mole of \(\mathrm{HCOO}^{-}\)after the reaction; and \(\mathrm{Q}\) is the total amount of the consumed charge.
The FEs of gas products \(\left(\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}}\right)\) were calculated according to Eq. (3):
\[
F_{\mathrm{H_2/CO/C_2H_4}}=\frac{(10^{-3}\mathrm{mv}\%\times\mathrm{F}\times\mathrm{G}\times\mathrm{P})}{60\times\mathrm{R}\times\mathrm{T}\times\mathrm{i}}
\]
## 3. Results and discussion
The XRD spectra acquired for the Bi-500@C, \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{3}{-}500@C\), \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}{-}500@C\), and \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{7}{-}500@C\) samples are illustrated in Fig. 1. For com
\(\mathrm{Biln}_{3}{-}500@C\), the most prominent XRD feature in this sample arises at \(27.681^{\circ}\) (also observed in \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\)) and is attributed to the (310) crystal planes of cubic \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) by comparison to PDF#06–0312. Additional peaks are observed at 30.378 (also in \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\)), 32.778, 39.672, 41.583, 48.929, and \(61.797^{\circ}\). In order of increasing angle, these peaks are attributed to the (222), (321), (420), (332), (520) and (630) crystal planes of cubic \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (\(\nu\), PDF#06–0312). By comparison, the spectrum of \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) displays an additional strong peak at \(29.155^{\circ}\) (less prominent in the previous case), i.e. the (311) facet of the cubic \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and a series of diffraction features arising at 30.580, 35.466, 51.037, and \(60.676^{\circ}\) which are ascribed to the (222), (440), (440), and (622) planes of cubic \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) (\(\nu\), PDF#06–0416). As for \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) and
\(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}{-}500@C\), the strongest feature in the XRD spectra of the \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{7^{-}}\) \(500@C\) samples also corresponds to the \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) (310) crystal plane. From the analysis of the results, it can be concluded that \(\mathrm{Bi}{-}500@C\) sample was mainly composed of \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) while \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{3}{-}500@C\), \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}{-}500@C\) and \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{7}{-}500@C\) samples contained a mixture \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) as the main phases. \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) diffraction peaks detected in the In-doped samples appear shifted to lower angles when compared to those of the pure \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) sample. This is likely due to the formation of a solid solution with In, which has a smaller ionic radius than Bi (Fig. S2) and is therefore responsible for the shift in the XRD features.
Fig. 2 (a) and (b) show the SEM and TEM images of the \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}\) - \(500@C\) sample. For completeness, the micrographs of undoped Bi-MOF are also reported in Fig. S3; these show \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) nanoparticles, of around \(200~\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter, deposited on the dendritic C framework. By comparison, the \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}{-}500@C\) sample also reveals the presence of smaller \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) particles \(\left(50\ \mathrm{nm}\right)\) as visible in Fig. 2 (a) and (b). The incorporation of these \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) nanoparticles into the Bi-MOF material is particularly important under the catalytic point of view as it likely increases the total active surface area of the catalyst and provides additional active sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER}\), which is expected to improve the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER}\) catalytic efficiency. It was however observed that the \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) nanoparticles tended to agglomerate upon the increase of the amount of In incorporated in the material, for instance to \(7\%\) of In, as illustrated by the micrographs displayed in Fig. S3. Agglomeration of these nanoparticles may have a negative impact on the catalytic activity of the material, as this phenomenon is expected to reduce the total surface area of the catalyst. In order to evaluate the surface area more accurately,
isothermal adsorption–desorption tests were carried out on all samples and their specific surface area was then calculated using BET equation (Fig. S4). The results of this analysis are reported in Table. S2. Firstly, it was determined that the specific surface area of \(\mathrm{Bi}{-}500@C\) is \(90.03\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). When In is incorporated, an increased in the surface area is indeed observed at first. For instance, this is determined to be \(110.15\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{3}{-}500@C\) and is further increased to \(151.54\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}{-}500@C\) – i.e. when the In ratio is increased to \(5\%\), a 1.5-fold increase in the surface area of the catalyst is observed cf. the bare \(\mathrm{Bi}{-}500@C\) sample. As anticipated, however, further incorporation of In led to a decrease of surface area, as exemplified by the \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{7}{-}500@C\) (7% In) wherein a value of \(82.00\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) was determined. The BET results are consistent with the observations based on the SEM micrographs and their analysis. EDS corroborated the SEM results (v. Fig. 2 (c)) – i.e. both larger \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) and smaller \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) nanoparticles were anchored to the carbon framework. Furthermore, HRTEM reported in Fig. 2(d) and (e) revealed lattice fringes corresponding to the \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) and to \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\), respectively. More specifically these correspond to the (310), (332), and (311) planes of \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}0_{3}\) (0.3220, 0.2170 and \(0.3040\ \mathrm{nm}\) apart as in Fig. 2(d) and (e)) and (222) and (400) planes of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}\) (0.2910 and \(0.2529\ \mathrm{nm}\) apart, as noted in Fig. 2 (e)).
High resolution XPS spectra of all the samples were acquired to unravel the oxidation states of Bi and In in our samples. The XPS results and analysis for \(\mathrm{Bi}{-}500@C\) and \(\mathrm{BiIn}_{5}{-}500@C\) are illustrated in Fig. 3, while the equivalent spectra for all other samples are reported in Fig. S5– S8. The peak energies of all features observed in each sample are tabulated in Table. S4. We will start our discussion with Fig. 3(a). In the region between 156 and \(168\;\mathrm{eV}\), identical
sets of symmetric peaks dominate the spectra of the \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\) (top panel in Fig. 3(a)) and \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) (bottom of Fig. 3(a)). These features arise at 158.85 and \(164.18\,\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\) and at 158.97 and \(164.30\,\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\), and are attributed to the \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) (at higher energies) and \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) (at lower energies) electronic states of Bi [2,5]. More specifically, the peak positions are consistent with the electronic states of \(\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}\), thus it can be inferred that the oxidation state of Bi in \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\) and \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) is \(+\,3\). The 4f peaks of Bi in spectra of \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) arise at slightly higher energies (by \(0.12\,\mathrm{eV}\)) when compared to the bare \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\), which confirms that In was indeed incorporated into the \(\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) lattice and thus affected the Bi chemical environment. The atomic ratio between Bi and In is shown in Table. S2. Furthermore, comparison with the XPS spectra in Fig. S5-S8 and the corresponding values in Table. S4 reveals that these peaks arise at the highest binding energies in \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) when compared to all other samples. The XPS spectrum recorded between 454 and \(442\,\mathrm{eV}\) reveals peaks attributed to the 3d state of In, as a confirmation that In was indeed incorporated in the \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) material (Fig. 3(b)). More specifically, the spectral features can be assigned to \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) by its characteristics \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) XPS peaks at 444.5 and \(452.0\,\mathrm{eV}\), respectively [4]. The spectra between 526 and \(538\,\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\) and \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) are reported for comparison at the top and bottom panels of Fig. 3 (c), respectively. In \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\), the three peaks from low binding energy to high binding energy belong to Bi-O bond, oxygen vacancy and \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\,=\,\mathsf{C}\) respectively. The XPS spectrum of \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) displays two prominent peaks at 529.58 and \(531.56\,\mathrm{eV}\), which are attributed to the M-O (M stands for metal) and oxygen vacancy, respectively [52,53]. An additional peak arising at \(\sim533\,\mathrm{eV}\) was detected and is ascribed to the oxygen atom in the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}=\mathsf{C}\). The increase of \(\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{O}\) bond peak area is due to the formation of In-O bond. This typical G \((1592\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) and D \((1341\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) band is attributed to the hexagonal bonded carbon atoms in the graphite network and the distorted carbon structure at the defect location, respectively (Fig. S9). The \(\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}\) of \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) catalyst is 0.82, which is less than \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\) \((\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}=0.83)\), indicating that the graphitization degree in the carbon layer is high, which is conducive to electron transport. The band of \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\) catalyst at \(309\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponds to Bi-O bond [54]. The shoulder around \(438\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) also be observed at \(\mathsf{B i-500@C}\). The Raman peaks of commercial \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) are located at 308, 365, 504 and \(637\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) respectively [55]. The peak of \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) catalyst at \(311\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is the superposition of Bi-O and In-O bonds. The vibration caused by In-O bond in \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) structure further confirmed the formation of In-O bond. The shoulder peak at \(464\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) catalyst also has a certain offset relative to Bi-500@C catalyst due to the influence of In-O bonds, which is the phonon confinement effect of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [56]. Fig. S10 shows the EPR spectrum measured at room temperature. It is found that \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) catalyst has a richer signal at \(\mathbf{g}\,=\,2.002\), suggesting that rich oxygen vacancies exist in \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C\) compared with Bi-500@C catalyst. Oxygen vacancies can coordinately accommodate unsaturated sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecular adsorption thus facilitating chemical reactions which would otherwise have high activation energies. Thus, the increase of oxygen vacancies is also expected to result in the improvement in the catalyst performance for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{ER}\).
