In order to first attest the electrochemical activity of all catalysts, LSV studies were undertaken and the corresponding results are illustrated in Fig. 4 and S11. The LSV results show the following trend in the electrochemical activity for the of \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{\mathsf{X}}{-}500@C\) samples: \(\mathsf{B i l n}_{5^{-}}500@C>\mathsf{B i l n}_{3^{-}}500@C>\mathsf{B i-500@C}>\mathsf{B i l n}_{7^{-}}500@C\). This trend may be understood on the basis of the materials characteri
for this catalyst shows a volcanic growth trend with the increase of potential. Indeed, the \(\mathrm{FE_{HCOOH}}\) was the highest \((97.5\%)\) at \(-\ 0.86\ \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}\) and decreased to \(84.8\%\) at \(-\ 1.16\ \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}\) . On the contrary, the current density increased to \(-\ 19.98\ \mathrm{mA\cm}^{-2}\) . Longevity without efficiency lost is a crucial catalyst property for practical applications. Thus, to evaluate \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) stability, we conducted a \(15~\mathrm{h}\) electrolysis test. At the end of this experiment, the constant potential was stable and equal to \(-\ 0.86\ \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}\) . The \(\mathrm{FE_{HCOOH}}\) remained above \(90\%\) (Fig. 4(f)). These results confirmed the longterm reliability of our \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) catalyst. In addition, LSV test and XRD characterization were carried out on the \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) catalyst after \(15~\mathrm{h}\) stability test (Fig. S14-15). The results show that neither the current density nor the phase of the material underwent any obvious changes.
The electron transfer process from the catalyst to the adsorbate was studied by impedance spectroscopy. As shown in Fig. S16 the \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) catalyst exhibits two distinct semiarches, corresponding to the stepwise electron/proton transfers with charge transfer resistances \(\mathbf{R}_{c t}^{\prime}\) and \(\mathbf{R}_{c t}^{\prime\prime}\) , respectively [57]. Due to the good \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption performance of the catalyst system, the \(\mathbf{R}_{c t}^{\prime}\) values of the first electron reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\cdot}\) of all catalysts are very similar. However, the subsequent second electron reduction showed different resistances, with the resistance of \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}{-}500@C\) catalyst being the lowest. As such, it is ascertained that the \(\mathrm{Biln}_{5}.\) - \(500@C\) promotes further conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\cdot}\) to HCOOH [58] more favorably than the other catalysts.
